-Traditional CNC motion planning is commonly organized as a sequential process, where trajectory smoothing is carried out before feedrate planning. Trajectory smoothing improves the geometric continuity of discrete program segments. Local smoothing methods construct smooth transition curves between adjacent segments. For example, Li et al.~\cite{Li2023} proposed a motion-overlap method for mixed line--arc tool paths, where overlapping transitions between adjacent motion segments improve connection continuity. Zhao et al.~\cite{Zhao2013} used curvature-continuous B-spline transitions for real-time look-ahead interpolation of short line segments, thereby improving the geometric continuity of corner transitions. Global methods instead reconstruct the discrete path over the entire domain. Song et al.~\cite{Song2021} combined control-point allocation with FIR filtering for global smoothing of continuous short-line tool paths. Sun et al.~\cite{Sun2024} studied a smooth interpolation method for five-axis machining paths to improve interpolation efficiency on complex paths. Feedrate planning then generates feedrate profiles along a prescribed geometric trajectory. Lee et al.~\cite{Lee2011} examined feedrate scheduling in NURBS interpolation. Erkorkmaz and Altintas~\cite{Erkorkmaz2001} improved dynamic smoothness in high-speed CNC machining through jerk-limited trajectory generation and quintic spline interpolation. Song et al.~\cite{Song2020} developed an adaptive feedrate-planning method for long five-axis spline paths using a look-ahead window and axis-drive constraints.
